  17:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c **** /** \file fsIsr.c
  18:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *
  19:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *  \ingroup  <DOXY_GROUP_FSF_STACK>
  20:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *
  21:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *  \brief    Implementation of package fsIsr (interrupt check) of library \n
  22:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *            functional safety.
  23:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *
  24:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *  \details  Runtime and frequency of interrupts will be\n
  25:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *            checked and compared against upper and lower limits.
  26:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  27:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  28:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Functional Safety Interrupt Supervision Component - Top-Level Design</b>
  29:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  30:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  31:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\li<b>Package Model </b>\n
ARM GAS  C:\Users\SX2605~1.HOD\AppData\Local\Temp\cccNF2gD.s 			page 2


  32:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\image html Package_Isr.jpg
  33:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  34:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Description </b>\n
  35:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *The fsIsr package provides the parts outlined in the model. By compilation switch, the Isr task h
  36:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *either in the usual operating mode or in test harness mode. When running in the usual operating m
  37:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *harness part will not be linked to the package. When running in test harness mode, stub services 
  38:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *the real Isr interfaces are separated from being called and only the test server will be able to 
  39:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  40:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Rationale </b>\n
  41:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *By the test harness part, it is possible to test all functions inside the Isr handler fully isola
  42:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *parts of the whole system. This is an important issue, otherwise it would be very difficult to te
  43:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *asynchronous level.
  44:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  45:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  46:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  47:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\li<b>State Model </b>\n
  48:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\image html StateModel_Isr.jpg
  49:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  50:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Description </b>\n
  51:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *Checking interrupts on every call of the generic is not efficient, thus an artificial state, call
  52:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *has been added. So, a check takes place on eveny 65 milliseconds. When elapsed, the Isr task hand
  53:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *states, one per call of the generic. If all states have been passed, the 65 milliseonds condition
  54:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *the Isr task handler does a transition back into state "No Operation".
  55:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  56:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Rationale </b>\n
  57:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *The Isr task handler does not have a its own initialisation routine. A pre-scheduling mechanism i
  58:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *the generic which has to be configured sufficiently by a concrete application. At its end, it mus
  59:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *interrupt events can be measured correctly.
  60:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  61:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  62:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  63:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\li<b>Scenario: Interrupt Supervision </b>\n
  64:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\image html SequenceChart_InterruptSupervision.jpg
  65:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  66:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Description </b>\n
  67:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *Its clear, this scenario initially starts in state "No Operation", and it has to be noticed that 
  68:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *and are not disabled during the whole process. They get disabled shortly when taking data. Each t
  69:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *calls the Isr task handler, the Isr task handler passes the state it is actually in, performs its
  70:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *finally, for all kind of interrupts, it notifies ongoing to the generic.
  71:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  72:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<b>Rationale </b>\n
  73:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *In an operational system, interrupts occur on every moment in time. So, the Ist task handler is a
  74:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *its measurement. For that raeson, there is no successful notification to the generic and, by a co
  75:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *Isr channel are not allowed to configure the run time limitation.
  76:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  77:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  78:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  79:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\li<b>Input Document Reference(s) </b>\n
  80:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *\n
  81:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<TABLE style="text-align: center">
  82:common/prog/FuncSafe/fsIsr.c ****  *<TR style="background-color:rgb(255,255,206)">
